# Term-to-polynomial compiler: symbolic terms over the polynomial
# vocabulary compile to the canonical multivariate normal forms of
# polynomial.zph -- pure forward-chaining rules, Trigger / Decompose /
# Assemble / Connect.
#
# PREREQUISITES:
#   .import decimal-arithmetic  # or binary-arithmetic / binary-nand-arithmetic
#   .import integer-arithmetic  # incl. the uniform operator facade
#   .import polynomial
#   .import topoly
#
# symbolic-core and diff are deliberately NOT prerequisites: this module
# consumes only the shared term VOCABULARY -- the binary operators
# +, -, *, the application (neg of U), sort declarations (~ symvar,
# ~ symconst) and numerals -- all of which are ordinary facts that exist
# with or without the simplifier. They are, however, the typical
# producers of the terms being compiled (simplified derivatives from
# diff + symbolic-minus compile directly).
#
# VOCABULARY and leaf handling:
#   - symvar X            compiles to (X poly ((pos zint &0) cons
#                         ((pos zint &1) cons nil))) -- representing the
#                         polynomial X
#   - symconst C          compiles identically: opaque constants are
#                         treated as INDETERMINATES in this context
#                         (identities hold within the polynomial ring
#                         Z[C, X, ...]); just like any variable, C must
#                         be listed in the pouter order; declared
#                         equations for C are ignored (refer to the TI
#                         rules below)
#   - zint numerals       compile to themselves with the magnitude in
#                         canonical form, (neg zint &0) maps to the zero
#                         polynomial (pos zint &0)
#   - natural numerals    promote to (pos zint N), with N in canonical
#                         form -- thus, subtraction that is partial over
#                         naturals becomes total here:
#                         (:topoly (&3 - &5)) = (neg zint &2)
#   - (U + V), (U - V), (U * V), (U ^ N), (neg of U)
#                         are routed to padd / psub / pmul / ppow / pneg
# Any other case -- division, other function applications, undeclared
# atoms, cons lists not representing numerals, zints whose magnitude is
# not a numeral -- gets no aspoly fact, and the request stays silently
# unanswered: partiality by absence.
#
# REQUEST IDIOM (testprime pattern, repeatable):
#   (:topoly T) = P
#
# IDENTITY CHECKING: canonical forms are unique hash-consed nodes, so
# proving a polynomial identity means compiling both sides and
# comparing NODE IDENTITY -- e.g. ((&1 + x) * (&1 - x)) and
# (&1 - (x * x)) compile to the same node. Consequently, the elimination
# of equal symbolic terms occurs here, across entire polynomials:
# (:topoly ((x + y) - (y + x))) yields (pos zint &0), a term that
# symbolic-minus's local X - X rule preserves unchanged, as it only
# cancels identical nodes.
#
# VARIABLE ORDER: as in polynomial.zph, cross-variable results require
# a declared strict total pouter order over all indeterminates in use;
# missing order pairs derive nothing.
#
# CANONICITY: leaves compile to canonical polynomials, and the
# delegated operations preserve canonicity, so every aspoly result is
# canonical; = is single-valued because each term shape is matched by
# exactly one assemble family and the data layer's = facts are
# single-valued.
#
# Display sugar: no registrations needed. (T topoly T) is a genuine
# request (:topoly T), needstopoly is a marker, and (P aspoly P) for a
# leaf genuinely reads "P is its own polynomial form" -- exactly like
# (T canon T).

.import polynomial

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rule TT0 -- Trigger
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T topoly T) => (T needstopoly T)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules TD -- Decompose (closed operator set)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((U + V) needstopoly (U + V)) => (U needstopoly U)
((U + V) needstopoly (U + V)) => (V needstopoly V)
((U - V) needstopoly (U - V)) => (U needstopoly U)
((U - V) needstopoly (U - V)) => (V needstopoly V)
((U * V) needstopoly (U * V)) => (U needstopoly U)
((U * V) needstopoly (U * V)) => (V needstopoly V)
((U ^ N) needstopoly (U ^ N)) => (U needstopoly U)
((neg of U) needstopoly (neg of U)) => (U needstopoly U)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules TB -- Base cases: leaves
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(X needstopoly X, X ~ symvar)
=> (X aspoly (X poly ((pos zint &0) cons ((pos zint &1) cons nil))))

(X needstopoly X, X ~ symconst)
=> (X aspoly (X poly ((pos zint &0) cons ((pos zint &1) cons nil))))

# A zint serves as its own constant polynomial when its
# magnitude meets the criteria of a numeral, as determined by the numeral
# test in common-arithmetic, and the magnitude is taken in its canonical
# form (canonnum): the numeral <01> is equivalent to &1, and an identity
# between the two is established as proven, not disproven. The expression
# (neg zint &0) yields zero with a sign it cannot possess, and it compiles
# to the zero polynomial; the simplifier, in the same manner, transforms it
# into &0. If taken at face value, 0 and (neg zint &0) would have generated
# distinct nodes, resulting in an identity that is disproven.
((G zint (A cons R)) needstopoly (G zint (A cons R)))
=> ((A cons R) needsnumeral (A cons R))
((G zint (A cons R)) needstopoly (G zint (A cons R)), (A cons R) isnumeral true)
=> ((A cons R) needscanon (A cons R))
((pos zint (A cons R)) needstopoly (pos zint (A cons R)), (A cons R) isnumeral true,
 (A cons R) canonnum N)
=> ((pos zint (A cons R)) aspoly (pos zint N))
((neg zint (A cons R)) needstopoly (neg zint (A cons R)), (A cons R) isnumeral true,
 (A cons R) canonnum N, N != &0)
=> ((neg zint (A cons R)) aspoly (neg zint N))
((neg zint (A cons R)) needstopoly (neg zint (A cons R)), (A cons R) isnumeral true,
 (A cons R) canonnum &0)
=> ((neg zint (A cons R)) aspoly (pos zint &0))

# A cons list qualifies as a constant precisely when it constitutes a
# numeral, as determined by common-arithmetic's numeral test, and it
# compiles to its canonical form, just as a zint magnitude does. Any list
# that fails to be a numeral acquires no aspoly fact, therefore a request
# concerning it remains unanswered, and an identity involving it gets no
# verdict. The numeral test is applied first since the canonical form
# strips trailing zero cells from any list, such as <x 0> becoming <x>.
((A cons R) needstopoly (A cons R)) => ((A cons R) needsnumeral (A cons R))
((A cons R) needstopoly (A cons R), (A cons R) isnumeral true)
=> ((A cons R) needscanon (A cons R))
((A cons R) needstopoly (A cons R), (A cons R) isnumeral true, (A cons R) canonnum N)
=> ((A cons R) aspoly (pos zint N))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules TA -- Assemble: delegate to the data layer (MA1/MA2 pattern --
# seed the operation as an ordinary fact, adopt its = result)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((U + V) needstopoly (U + V), U aspoly P, V aspoly Q) => (P padd Q)
((U + V) needstopoly (U + V), U aspoly P, V aspoly Q, (P padd Q) = R)
=> ((U + V) aspoly R)

((U - V) needstopoly (U - V), U aspoly P, V aspoly Q) => (P psub Q)
((U - V) needstopoly (U - V), U aspoly P, V aspoly Q, (P psub Q) = R)
=> ((U - V) aspoly R)

((U * V) needstopoly (U * V), U aspoly P, V aspoly Q) => (P pmul Q)
((U * V) needstopoly (U * V), U aspoly P, V aspoly Q, (P pmul Q) = R)
=> ((U * V) aspoly R)

((U ^ N) needstopoly (U ^ N), U aspoly P) => (P ppow N)
((U ^ N) needstopoly (U ^ N), U aspoly P, (P ppow N) = R) => ((U ^ N) aspoly R)

((neg of U) needstopoly (neg of U), U aspoly P) => (P pneg P)
((neg of U) needstopoly (neg of U), U aspoly P, (P pneg P) = R)
=> ((neg of U) aspoly R)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rule TC0 -- Connect: expose the result under =, testprime-style
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T topoly T, T aspoly P) => ((T topoly T) = P)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules TI -- polynomial identity (A ≡ B): compile both sides and compare
# normal forms. Canonical forms are unique hash-consed nodes, so the
# comparison IS node identity -- expressed through unification: the SAME
# variable P in both result conditions binds only if both compilations
# reached the identical node. Repeatable query idiom: (A ≡ B) = proven.
#
# DISPROOF needs no negation. Once both sides have a normal form they
# either are the same node or they are not, and != decides it directly --
# so the verdict stays inside the positive stratum, independent of the
# order in which the compile state saturates. What remains partial is the
# case where a side has NO normal form (an undeclared atom, an operator
# outside the vocabulary): then neither verdict rule fires and the request
# is unanswered. That is the honest reading -- "I could not compile this"
# is not "these differ".
#
# Hence a three-way outcome: proven, disproven, or silence. The two
# verdicts are mutually exclusive by construction, so = stays
# single-valued.
#
# Both verdicts pertain to polynomials: each symvar and symconst
# functions as an independent indeterminate, and a declared equation
# like (r * r) = &2 remains unexamined, even though symbolic-core's
# knowledge folding draws upon it. Thus, disproven says that the two
# sides are distinct as polynomials, not that they differ for the
# values the indeterminates stand for. Proven remains sound under any
# such equation: an identity in the free ring continues to hold in
# every quotient of it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(A ≡ B) => (:topoly A)
(A ≡ B) => (:topoly B)
(A ≡ B, (:topoly A) = P, (:topoly B) = P) => ((A ≡ B) = proven)
(A ≡ B, (:topoly A) = P, (:topoly B) = Q, P != Q) => ((A ≡ B) = disproven)
